  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Total federal contract obligations in Oklahoma are $4.1B for FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 39.3% year over year. Award activity in the state includes 17,968 awards with an average award size of $230K. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $2.7B so far this fiscal year. Federal buying outside the Defense sector includes the Department of Transportation at $567M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $206M. The vendor list is led by THE BOEING COMPANY at $770M, followed by THE BOEING COMPANY at $168M and V2X AEROSPACE LLC at $119M. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations at $711M, with Engineering Services at $379M and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $293M. Fiscal-year comparisons show FY25 obligations at $6.8B and FY24 obligations at $5.5B, while earlier years include FY21 at $4.5B and FY22 at $5B. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers in Oklahoma this fiscal year?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $2.7B, making Defense the dominant buyer. The Department of Transportation obligated $567M and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $206M. The General Services Administration obligated $200M and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $152M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries capture the most federal dollars in Oklahoma?",
        "body": "THE BOEING COMPANY is the top vendor with $770M, followed on the vendor list by THE BOEING COMPANY again at $168M and V2X AEROSPACE LLC at $119M. Aircraft Manufacturing leads industries with $711M, followed by Engineering Services at $379M and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $293M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction represents $260M of obligations and Facilities Support Services represents $193M."
      }
    ]
  },
